<section class="col-xs-12 col-sm-12 col-md-12 col-lg-9 col-xl-9" id="main">
<div class="html" id="tag">
<h3 class="tex-subsection" id="subsection:initial-objects"><span data-tag="02H6">4.7.3</span> Initial and Final Objects</h3>
<p>
Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a category. Recall that an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>final</em> if, for every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, there is a unique morphism from $C$ to $X$. This definition has an obvious counterpart in the setting of $\infty $-categories. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="02H7">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02H7">Definition <span data-tag="02H7">4.7.3.1</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. We say that an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>final</em> if, for every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)$ is a contractible Kan complex. We say that $X$ is <em>initial</em> if, for every object $D \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,D)$ is a contractible Kan complex. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="06QZ">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06QZ">Remark <span data-tag="06QZ">4.7.3.2</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. Then an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is final (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="02H7" href="/tag/02H7">4.7.3.1</a>) if and only if it is $(-2)$-truncated (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="05EC" href="/tag/05EC">4.7.1.1</a>). If this condition is satisfied, then $X$ is $n$-truncated for every integer $n$ (Remark <a data-tag="06QT" href="/tag/06QT">4.7.1.3</a>). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="02H8">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02H8">Remark <span data-tag="02H8">4.7.3.3</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. Then an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is initial if and only if it is final when viewed as an object of the opposite $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}}$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="02MX">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02MX">Example <span data-tag="02MX">4.7.3.4</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. Then the cone point of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\triangleright }$ is a final object. Similarly, the cone point of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\triangleleft }$ is an initial object. See Example <a data-tag="02MW" href="/tag/02MW">4.7.1.11</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="02H9">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02H9">Example <span data-tag="02H9">4.7.3.5</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a category. An object $Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is final if and only if it is final when viewed as an object of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. Similarly, an object $Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is initial if and only if it is initial when viewed as an object of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="02HB">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02HB">Example <span data-tag="02HB">4.7.3.6</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a locally Kan simplicial category and let $X$ be an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then $X$ is final as an object of the homotopy coherent nerve $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{hc}}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ if and only if, for each object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the Kan complex $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)_{\bullet }$ is contractible. Similarly, $X$ is initial if and only if for each object $D \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the Kan complex $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,D)_{\bullet }$ is contractible. See Example <a data-tag="06QV" href="/tag/06QV">4.7.1.7</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="06R0">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06R0">Example <span data-tag="06R0">4.7.3.7</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a Kan complex, which we regard as an object of the $\infty $-category of spaces $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ (Construction <a data-tag="00TZ" href="/tag/00TZ">3.1.6.1</a>). Then $X$ is an initial object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ if and only if it is empty, and a final object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ if and only if it is contractible. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="02HE">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02HE">Proposition <span data-tag="02HE">4.7.3.8</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a differential graded category, so that the differential graded nerve $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{dg}}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is an $\infty $-category (Theorem <a data-tag="00PW" href="/tag/00PW">2.5.3.10</a>). Let $X$ be an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The object $X$ is initial when viewed as an object of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{dg}}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The object $X$ is final when viewed as an object of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{dg}}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The identity morphism $\operatorname{id}_{X}: X \rightarrow X$ is nullhomologous: that is, there exists a $1$-chain $e \in \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,X)_{1}$ satisfying $\partial (e) = \operatorname{id}_{X}$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We will show that $(2) \Leftrightarrow (3)$; the proof that $(1) \Leftrightarrow (3)$ is similar. The implication $(2) \Rightarrow (3)$ follows from Remark <a data-tag="05EL" href="/tag/05EL">4.7.1.15</a> and Example <a data-tag="06QW" href="/tag/06QW">4.7.1.9</a>. Conversely, suppose that there exists $e \in \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,X)_{1}$ satisfying $\partial (e) = \operatorname{id}_{X}$. For every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, $e$ determines a chain homotopy from the identity map $\operatorname{id}: \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)_{\ast } \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)_{\ast }$ to the zero map. It follows that the homology of chain complex $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)_{\ast }$ vanishes, so that the Eilenberg-MacLane space $\mathrm{K}( \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)_{\ast } )$ of Construction <a data-tag="00QT" href="/tag/00QT">2.5.6.3</a> is a contractible Kan complex. Combining Example <a data-tag="01L9" href="/tag/01L9">4.6.5.14</a> with Proposition <a data-tag="01L5" href="/tag/01L5">4.6.5.10</a>, we conclude that the morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{dg}}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }(C,X)$ is also contractible. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="02HS">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02HS">Proposition <span data-tag="02HS">4.7.3.9</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be an equivalence of $\infty $-categories and let $X$ be an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then $X$ is an initial object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ if and only if $F(X)$ is an initial object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. Similarly, $X$ is a final object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ if and only if $F(X)$ is a final object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Apply Remark <a data-tag="05EJ" href="/tag/05EJ">4.7.1.14</a> in the special case $n = -2$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="02MY">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02MY">Corollary <span data-tag="02MY">4.7.3.10</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be $\infty $-categories which are equivalent. Then: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ has an initial object if and only if the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ has an initial object. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ has a final object if and only if the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ has a final object. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
We now show that if an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits a final (or initial) object, then that object is uniquely determined up to (canonical) isomorphism. This is a consequence of the following reformulation of Definition <a data-tag="02H7" href="/tag/02H7">4.7.3.1</a>: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="06R1">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06R1">Proposition <span data-tag="06R1">4.7.3.11</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. An object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is final if and only if, for every integer $m \geq 1$ and every morphism of simplicial sets $\sigma : \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^ m \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ satisfying $\sigma (m) = X$, there exists an $m$-simplex $\overline{\sigma }: \Delta ^ m \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ satisfying $\overline{\sigma }|_{ \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^ m} = \sigma $. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Apply Proposition <a data-tag="05ET" href="/tag/05ET">4.7.1.16</a> in the special case $n = -2$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="06R2">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06R2">Corollary <span data-tag="06R2">4.7.3.12</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $X$ be an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The object $X$ is initial if and only if the projection map $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{X/} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a trivial Kan fibration of simplicial sets. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The object $X$ is final if and only if the projection map $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a trivial Kan fibration of simplicial sets. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assertion $(2)$ is a reformulation of Proposition <a data-tag="06R1" href="/tag/06R1">4.7.3.11</a>, and $(1)$ follows by a similar argument. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="02HJ">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02HJ">Corollary <span data-tag="02HJ">4.7.3.13</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a Kan complex and let $x \in X$ be a vertex. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The vertex $x$ is initial when viewed as an object of the $\infty $-category $X$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The vertex $x$ is final when viewed as an object of the $\infty $-category $X$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The Kan complex $X$ is contractible. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> In particular, these conditions are independent of the choice of vertex $x \in X$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      If the Kan complex $X$ is contractible, then the projection map $X_{/x} \rightarrow X$ is a trivial Kan fibration (Corollary <a data-tag="0192" href="/tag/0192">4.3.7.19</a>), so the object $x \in X$ is final by virtue of Corollary <a data-tag="06R2" href="/tag/06R2">4.7.3.12</a>. Conversely, if the projection map $X_{/x} \rightarrow X$ is a trivial Kan fibration, then it is a homotopy equivalence (Proposition <a data-tag="00U9" href="/tag/00U9">3.1.7.10</a>). Since the Kan complex $X_{/x}$ is contractible (Corollary <a data-tag="018Y" href="/tag/018Y">4.3.7.14</a>), it follows that $X$ is contractible. This proves the equivalence of $(2) \Leftrightarrow (3)$; the equivalence of $(1) \Leftrightarrow (3)$ follows by a similar argument. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="02HM">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02HM">Corollary <span data-tag="02HM">4.7.3.14</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\mathrm{init}} \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be the full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ spanned by the initial objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\mathrm{fin}} \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be the full subcategory spanned by the final objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ contains an initial object, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\mathrm{init}}$ is a contractible Kan complex. If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ contains a final object, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ is a contractible Kan complex. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ contains a final object, we will show that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ is a contractible Kan complex (the analogous assertion for initial objects follows by a similar argument). Suppose we are given a morphism of simplicial sets $\sigma : \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^ n \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\mathrm{fin} }$; we wish to show that $\sigma $ can be extended to a morphism $\overline{\sigma }: \Delta ^ n \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$. If $n=0$, this follows from our assumption that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ contains a final object. If $n &gt; 0$, then we can regard $\sigma $ as a morphism from $\operatorname{\partial \Delta }^ n$ to $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ with the property that $\sigma (i) \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is final for $0 \leq i \leq n$. Applying Proposition <a data-tag="06R1" href="/tag/06R1">4.7.3.11</a>, we conclude that $\sigma $ can be extended to a morphism $\overline{\sigma }: \Delta ^{n} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, which automatically factors through the full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\mathrm{fin}} \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-notation" id="02HP">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02HP">Notation <span data-tag="02HP">4.7.3.15</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. We will sometimes use the symbol ${\bf 1}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ to denote a final object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, provided that such an object exists. In this case, we will sometimes abuse terminology by referring to ${\bf 1}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ as <em>the</em> final object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. This abuse is justified by Corollary <a data-tag="02HM" href="/tag/02HM">4.7.3.14</a>, which guarantees that ${\bf 1}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ is uniquely determined up to a contractible space of choices (in particular, it is well-defined up to isomorphism). Similarly, we will often use the symbol $\emptyset _{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ to denote an initial object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (if such an object exists), and refer to it as <em>the</em> initial object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. When it is unlikely to cause confusion, we might omit the subscripts and denote the objects $\emptyset _{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ and ${\bf 1}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ by $\emptyset $ and ${\bf 1}$, respectively. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="02HN">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02HN">Corollary <span data-tag="02HN">4.7.3.16</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $X$ be a final object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then an object $Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is final if and only if it is isomorphic to $X$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $X$ and $Y$ are final objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, then they are contained in the contractible Kan complex $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ of Corollary <a data-tag="02HM" href="/tag/02HM">4.7.3.14</a> and are therefore isomorphic when viewed as objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Conversely, if $X$ and $Y$ are isomorphic, then Remark <a data-tag="05EG" href="/tag/05EG">4.7.1.13</a> guarantees that $Y$ is final. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="02HQ">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02HQ">Corollary <span data-tag="02HQ">4.7.3.17</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $X$ be an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
If $X$ is final as an object of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, then it is also final when viewed as an object of the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ has an final object and $X$ is final as an object of the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$, then $X$ is final as an object of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assertion $(1)$ is immediate from the definition. To prove $(2)$, assume that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ has a final object $Y$. Then $Y$ is also final when viewed as an object of the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$. If $X$ is a final object of $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$, then $X$ and $Y$ are isomorphic when viewed as objects of $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$, hence also when viewed as objects of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Invoking Corollary <a data-tag="02HN" href="/tag/02HN">4.7.3.16</a>, we conclude that $X$ is also a final object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-warning" id="02HR">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02HR">Warning <span data-tag="02HR">4.7.3.18</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing an object $X$ which is final as an object of the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$. Then $X$ need not be final when viewed as an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. For example, if $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is simply connected Kan complex, then every object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is final when viewed as an object of the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} = \pi _{\leq 1}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. However, $X$ is final as an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ only if $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is contractible (Corollary <a data-tag="02HJ" href="/tag/02HJ">4.7.3.13</a>). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="02HG">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02HG">Corollary <span data-tag="02HG">4.7.3.19</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $f: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a diagram, let $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be the projection map, and let $X$ be a final object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
There exists an object $\widetilde{X} \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/}$ satisfying $U( \widetilde{X} ) = X$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
If $\widetilde{X}$ is any object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/}$ satisfying $U( \widetilde{X} ) = X$, then $\widetilde{X}$ is a final object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/}$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assertion $(1)$ is equivalent to the statement that $f$ can be lifted to a diagram $\widetilde{f}: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X}$. This is clear, since the projection map $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a trivial Kan fibration (Corollary <a data-tag="06R2" href="/tag/06R2">4.7.3.12</a>). To prove $(2)$, fix an object $\widetilde{X} \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/}$ satisfying $U( \widetilde{X} ) = X$. By virtue of Corollary <a data-tag="06R2" href="/tag/06R2">4.7.3.12</a>, it will suffice to show that the projection map $(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/})_{/\widetilde{X} } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/}$ is a trivial Kan fibration. Equivalently, we wish to show that every lifting problem 
    <p>
<div class="equation" id="02HH">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="02HH" href="/tag/02HH">4.74</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:coslices-create-final} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ A \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  (\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/})_{/\widetilde{X}} \ar [d] \\ B \ar [r] \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] &amp;  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/} } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p>
       admits a solution, provided that the left vertical map is a monomorphism. Unwinding the definitions, we can rewrite (<a data-tag="02HH" href="/tag/02HH">4.74</a>) as a lifting problem 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ K \star A \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X} \ar [d] \\ K \star B \ar [r] \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] &amp;  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}. } \]
</div>
<p>
       Our assumption that the object $Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is final guarantees that this lifting problem has a solution (Corollary <a data-tag="06R2" href="/tag/06R2">4.7.3.12</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></p></article>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="02J2">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02J2">Proposition <span data-tag="02J2">4.7.3.20</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $f: X \rightarrow Y$ be a morphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $f$ is an isomorphism from $X$ to $Y$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (Definition <a data-tag="004R" href="/tag/004R">1.4.6.1</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $f$ is final when regarded as an object of the slice $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/Y}$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2')$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $f$ is final when regarded as an object of the oriented fiber product $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\vec{\times }_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \{ Y\} $. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $f$ is initial when regarded as an object of the coslice $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{X/}$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3')$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $f$ is initial when regarded as an object of the oriented fiber product $\{ X\}  \vec{\times }_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      The equivalences $(2) \Leftrightarrow (2')$ and $(3) \Leftrightarrow (3')$ follow from Corollary <a data-tag="02VT" href="/tag/02VT">4.6.4.20</a> and Proposition <a data-tag="02HS" href="/tag/02HS">4.7.3.9</a>. The equivalence $(1) \Leftrightarrow (2)$ follows from Example <a data-tag="05FD" href="/tag/05FD">4.7.2.5</a> and Proposition <a data-tag="05FF" href="/tag/05FF">4.7.2.7</a> (applied in the case $n=-2$), and the equivalence $(1) \Leftrightarrow (3)$ follows by applying the same argument to the opposite $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}}$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="02J4">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02J4">Corollary <span data-tag="02J4">4.7.3.21</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $X$ be an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The object $X$ is final if and only if the projection map $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits a section $G$ satisfying $G(X) = \operatorname{id}_{X}$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The object $X$ is initial if and only if the projection map $F': \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{X/} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits a section $G'$ satisfying $G'(X) = \operatorname{id}_{X}$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We will prove $(1)$; the proof of $(2)$ is similar. If $X$ is a final object, then the projection map $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a trivial Kan fibration (Corollary <a data-tag="06R2" href="/tag/06R2">4.7.3.12</a>), so the construction $X \mapsto \operatorname{id}_ X$ can be extended to a section of $F$. Conversely, suppose that $F$ admits a section $G: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X}$ satisfying $G(X) = \operatorname{id}_ X$. Let $C$ be an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$: we wish to show that the Kan complex $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)$ is contractible. The functors $G$ and $F$ induce morphisms of Kan complexes 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X) \xrightarrow {G} \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X}}( G(C), \operatorname{id}_ X ) \xrightarrow {F} \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( C,X), \]
</div>
<p>
       whose composition is the identity. In particular, the Kan complex $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)$ is a retract of $ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X}}( G(C), \operatorname{id}_ X )$. It will therefore suffice to show that the Kan complex $ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X}}( G(C), \operatorname{id}_ X )$ is contractible. This is clear, since $\operatorname{id}_{X}$ is a final object of the slice $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X}$ (Proposition <a data-tag="02J2" href="/tag/02J2">4.7.3.20</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="02J5">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02J5">Corollary <span data-tag="02J5">4.7.3.22</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $X$ be an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is final. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
There exists a functor $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ satisfying $F|_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} = \operatorname{id}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ and for which the composition 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \Delta ^1 \simeq \{ X\} ^{\triangleright } \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\triangleright } \xrightarrow {F} \operatorname{\mathcal{C}} \]
</div>
<p>
 is the identity morphism $\operatorname{id}_{X}$ (in particular, $F$ carries the cone point of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\triangleright }$ to the object $X$). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The inclusion map $\{ X\}  \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is right anodyne. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      The equivalence $(1) \Leftrightarrow (2)$ is a reformulation of Corollary <a data-tag="02J4" href="/tag/02J4">4.7.3.21</a>. We next show that $(2)$ implies $(3)$. If condition $(2)$ is satisfied, then we have a commutative diagram of simplicial sets 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \{ X\}  \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \{ X\} ^{\triangleright } \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \{ X\}  \ar [d] \\ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\triangleright } \ar [r]^-{F} &amp;  \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \]
</div>
<p>
       where the horizontal compositions are the identity. Since the inclusion $\{ X\} ^{\triangleright } \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\triangleright }$ is right anodyne (Lemma <a data-tag="0196" href="/tag/0196">4.3.7.8</a>), it follows that the inclusion $\{ X\}  \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is also right anodyne. 
    <p>
      We now complete the proof by showing that $(3)$ implies $(2)$. Suppose that the inclusion $\{ X\}  \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is right anodyne; we wish to show that there exists a functor $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ satisfying $F|_{ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} = \operatorname{id}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ and $F|_{ \{ X\} ^{\triangleright } } = \operatorname{id}_ X$. For this, it will suffice to show that the inclusion map 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\coprod _{ \{ X\}  } \{ X\} ^{\triangleright } \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\triangleright } \]
</div>
<p>
       is inner anodyne, which is a special case of Proposition <a data-tag="018J" href="/tag/018J">4.3.6.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></p></p></article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="02P2">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02P2">Corollary <span data-tag="02P2">4.7.3.23</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category which has either an initial object or a final object. Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is weakly contractible. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We will assume that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ has a final object $X$; the case where $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ has an initial object follows by a similar argument. Corollary <a data-tag="02J5" href="/tag/02J5">4.7.3.22</a> implies that the inclusion map $\{ X\}  \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is right anodyne. In particular, it is anodyne and therefore a weak homotopy equivalence. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
